in this video I want to talk a little  bit about diabetes and the difference  between type 1 and type 2 diabetes so  what is diabetes in a nutshell diabetes  basically just is high blood glucose  levels can be lowered so high blood  glucose levels are bad and they could be  damaging and the reason why they can be  damaging is because glucose will  actually stick on to red blood cells and  affect and interfere with the  circulation of your blood and that can  be particularly damaged damaging to a  lot of blood vessels and of course can  lead to a lot of problems because your  blood has to go everywhere in your body  so no area in your body is safe from  diabetes so what's the difference though  between type 1 and type 2 type 1 is  normally called juvenile onset let me  actually write this in green juvenile  onset and type 2 is usually called adult  onset now those terms are a little bit  or at least can be a little bit  misleading although they're it can be  helpful to remember some small details  but essentially the root difference is  that the problem with type 1 diabetics  is they have a loss of insulin  production they do not produce insulin  type 2 diabetics their problem is a  little bit different they don't have a  problem producing insulin they can  produce it but the they have an  inability there's of the cells to  respond to insulin so they have a  decreased sensitivity to insulin so type  1 diabetics have no insulin type 2  diabetics are insensitive to insulin so  their bodies their cells cannot respond  to the insulin levels even if the  insulin is there so what's the cause of  type 1 diabetes and what's the cause of  type 2 diabetes the cause of type 1  diabetes basically is damage to the  pancreas specifically the cells that  create insulin which are the beta cells  now the the cause can also have  something to do with genetics as well  keep in mind I am kind of simplifying  this overall and I'm not a doctor so  I'm not I'm not an expert all this but I  do have a general idea of what's going  on so the cause like I mentioned is the  damage that pancreas is beta cells so  that they the beta cells can no longer  produce insulin genetics play a role as  well  the cause is often on an autoimmune  disease so if basically if your immune  system attacks your own pancreas  specifically your beta cells and damages  them to a point where you can't create  insulin at all or not enough insulin  then that is a problem so typically this  happens with individuals who are you  know went out when they're pretty young  hence the term juvenile onset usually  people are born with type 1 diabetes or  they're pretty young when they have this  and it's diagnosed pretty early in their  lives whereas type 2 diabetics  it's called adult onset because  generally speaking a diet and lifestyle  have have to do with acquiring type 2  diabetes and again genetics play a role  with type 2 diabetics as well typically  though diets high in sugar high in fat  and high in cholesterol all sort of lead  to type type 2 diabetes and individuals  who are obese typically are more  susceptible to type 2 diabetes hence the  term adult onset because usually people  who get type 2 diabetics diabetes are  diagnosed later in life so what is the  treatment for type 1 diabetics versus  type 2 diabetics generally speaking both  of them are of course encouraged to have  a healthy diet and definitely exercise  this is true not just for diabetic  patients but just all people in general  right you're always want to promote  health by encouraging a healthy diet and  healthy exercise but with type 1  diabetics the treatment is often just  administering insulin and why is that  well type 1 diabetics don't have a  problem with responding to insulin they  just don't produce enough of it or don't  produce it at all so a lot of type 1  diabetics which you'll see is actually  had a buddy of mine who was diabetic and  they basically had an IV connected to  them at all times  so they would like continually  throughout the  a bee on a drip of insulin and their  body would get that insulin that they  needed and they would basically live a  normal life so if if type 1 diabetics  right they just don't have insulin or  don't have enough all they need is  insulin that's what they need to solve  their problem type 2 diabetics can you  administer them insulin generally  speaking no in some cases they might  just need a larger dose of insulin in  order to respond to it because they're  just decreased sensitivity to it but  generally speaking administering insulin  is not the best course of treatment here  what you if you are diabetic or know  someone who's diabetic you might be  familiar with this drug called metformin  so what metformin does is it's a drug  involved essentially in increasing the  sensitivity of cells to insulin so  basically it allows insulin to actually  work or be effective that's one of the  things it does it also has it also  decreases hepatic gluconeogenesis which  basically means that it causes the liver  to stop producing glucose because the  deliverer will produce glucose and start  dumping it into your blood so metformin  sort of inhibits that decreases that so  I hope that was a sort of good general  overview about the difference between  type 1 diabetics and type 2 diabetics I  hope that was a clear or an organized  differentiation between the two hope  that was helpful  I am a tutor if you live in Southern  California feel free to contact me via  email at Mufi University at gmail.com  thank you for watching
 

